# Auto-detect text files and normalize line endings to LF
* text=auto eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
data/reference/** text eol=lf
configs/** text eol=lf
# Audit §79.7 (N-154): the audit volumes are frozen by sha256, so their bytes are part of the
# verification instrument. Pin them to LF in the working copy as well as in the stored blob.
reports/audit/** text eol=lf
# Audit §86.8 item 1 (N-170): a compressed archive is bytes, not lines. Under `text=auto eol=lf`
# git auto-detected many of these containers as text and checkout rewrote their CRLF bytes, so a
# fresh clone reported every rewritten archive as modified and the green cache could never be
# reused. Attribute rules are last-match-wins, so these sit after every `text` rule above and
# store the object's own bytes.
*.csv.zip -text
*.json.gz -text
*.zip -text
*.gz -text
# Audit §96.2 → §97 (N-186): a captured statutory source under data/raw/circulars/<ref>/ is bytes, not
# lines. The NSE STT/turnover payload is an HTML document whose retrieved bytes carry CRLF, and
# `* text=auto eol=lf` rewrote it on checkout: the blob held 330,001 bytes / 91e8ace7… while its manifest
# and the four criteria rows it backs state the retrieved 330,003 / 5df41b04…. Attribute rules are
# last-match-wins, so this sits after every `text` rule and stores each captured payload's own bytes.
# The calendar circulars at data/raw/circulars/*.json are not matched: they are JSON, which the rule
# above already pins to LF.
data/raw/circulars/*/** -text

